What exactly was "the object" that was shot down over Alaska?
Confusing and contradictory, speculation rife.
The U.S. military on Friday afternoon (February 10) again used America's most advanced stealth jet F-22 to shoot down an unidentified object flying over the U.S. state of Alaska.
The location of the hit was ten miles from Alaska's coast.
This is the second time in less than a week that US forces have downed an airborne object.
On last Saturday, a spy balloon from China drifted over American airspace for several days before being destroyed near the coast of South Carolina in the United States.
The Pentagon said that the second object shot down entered US airspace for the first time on Thursday evening, was tracked by US military in the northeastern part of Alaska as it moved at an altitude of 12,000 meters (40,000 feet), and was about the size of a small car, much smaller than the previously shot-down Chinese spy balloon.
The Pentagon also believes there is no indication at this time that the object is manipulatable.
White House National Security Council Strategic Communications Coordinator John Coby said on Friday that while the exact appearance of the object in high-altitude flight is unclear, it poses a reasonable threat to the safety of civil aviation aircraft.
Kobe stated that the object is different from the spy balloon discovered in the United States, which did not have a payload hanging from it.
Kobe said, “We call it an ‘object’ is the best description we can make at this point.”
President Biden listened to Pentagon advice before ordering the destruction of the object and added that he made his decision “very carefully.”
The official description of this object by the US authorities can be summarized as follows: about the size of a small car, without control sticks, without suspension systems, and flying at an altitude of around 10,000 meters.
But these pieces of information still fail to answer fundamental questions, such as what this object is, which country it belongs to, and what task it is performing.
Currently, many analyses and speculations online suggest that this could be another Chinese balloon.
It may have come from China, like that spy balloon intruding American airspace and the Chinese balloon floating above Latin America, performing some task at different locations.
Others have linked this "object" from Alaska to unidentified flying objects (UFOs).
The report from Newsweek magazine cites former US Congressman Peter Meijer, who tweeted on Friday, “I really hope this ‘unidentified flying object’ is an espionage balloon rather than a legitimate UFO.”
The irony of conflating extraterrestrial spacecraft with weather balloons is striking, but it does not warrant an interstellar conflict, he said.
Meager tweeted this shortly after Kobe confirmed that the object in Alaska had been shot down.
The Weekly News reports that it is not just Major Gray who doubts the object to be a UFO.
Republican Representative Tim Burchett of Tennessee criticized the government's cover-up of intelligence last month, and is demanding that the Office of the Director of National Intelligence (DNI) release reports on "unidentified flying objects."
Iran Commemorates Revolution Anniversary as Hackers Sabotage President's TV Speeches in Support of Protests
The Islamic Republic of Iran held an official rally Saturday to commemorate the 44th anniversary of the Iranian Revolution, but anti-government hackers briefly interrupted President Ebrahim Raisi’s televised speech.
The President’s televised address was interrupted on the internet for about a minute, with a banner of hackers opposed to the Iranian government appearing on the screen. They go by the name “Edalat Ali (Ali Justice).”
And another voice shouted “Death to the Islamic Republic.”
Reuters today reports that Iran is celebrating its revolution anniversary and hackers have disrupted national TV broadcasts.
The official in Iran celebrated the 44th anniversary of the Iranian Revolution, and the hardline government of Rouhani faces the most daring challenge from young protesters demanding that they step down; however, Rouhani still called on “deceived youth” to repent so they can receive amnesty from Iran's supreme leaders.
After Mahsa Amini, 22, died in custody of Iran’s morality police on September 16, nationwide protests erupted across the country, still in Iran.
Iranian security forces carried out deadly repression of protests, which is one of the most powerful challenges faced by the Islamic Republic since the revolution in 1979 that overthrew the 2500-year-old monarchy.
As part of the amnesty to commemorate the anniversary of the revolution, the Iranian authorities released on Friday the imprisoned dissident Fahd Mesami and the Iranian-French scholar Fariba Adelha, who were on hunger strike.
Earlier, Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei issued a decree of amnesty that includes some prisoners arrested in recent anti-government protests.
According to the human rights organization HRANA, as of Friday, 528 protesters have been killed, including 71 minors.
The report says that 70 government security personnel were killed.
It is believed that up to 19,763 protesters were arrested.
For weeks, Iranian leaders and official media have been calling for voting at Saturday’s rally to show opposition to the protests and support for the government.
On Friday night, on the eve of the anniversary of the Iranian Revolution, the official media showed fireworks as part of a government-sponsored celebration, and people chanted "Allahu Akbar!"
(God is the greatest! )
However, one can also hear many people shouting “Death to the dictator!” at the rally.
And videos posted on social media of “The Death of Islamic Republic”.
Saturday, the government TV station broadcast live footage of official gatherings across all states.
In Tehran, as part of official celebrations, displays of indigenous anti-ballistic missiles, drones, and antisubmarine frigates and other military equipment were showcased.
Liesley’s speeches are frequently interrupted by “get out of America,” which is the hallmark slogan at state rallies.
The crowd chanted "Israel must die."
Reuters said that Adelkhah, who has been in prison since 2019, is one of seven French nationals detained in Iran, which is a factor in the deterioration of relations between Paris and Tehran over the past few months.
She was sentenced to five years in prison for national security charges in 2020.
They later confined her at home, but she returned to prison in January.
Adelkhah denies these charges.
And just a week before Mesa was released, supporters warned that he faced a risk of death due to his hunger strike.
He was arrested in 2018 for protesting mandatory headscarf wearing.
The French Foreign Ministry on Friday responded to Adelkhah’s release, calling for her freedom “including if she so desires, allowing her to return to France.”
