Q&A on Double Ninth Festival | How to View US Non-Farm Payrolls Data Surpassing Expectations in January and Future Trends - Economic Observer – A Professional Financial News Website
Q: How does Zhongyáng Investment view the large overshooting of January non-farm payroll employment data in the US and its future trend?
A: On February 3, the U.S. Department of Labor released the non-farm payroll data for January 2023, showing an increase of 5.17 million in non-farm jobs, significantly higher than the market expectation of 1.89 million; the unemployment rate fell to 3.4%, its lowest level in nearly 50 years; and hourly wages grew at a year-over-year rate of 4.4% and at a month-over-month rate of 0.3%, slightly slowing.
From a total perspective, this data is subject to many one-off and technical factors of disturbance, such as teachers returning from strike, adjustments in survey statistics, updates on seasonal adjustment factors, etc., which may be revised (refer to the downward revision of non-farm payroll employment for January 2022 from 5.04 million to 3.64 million). Non-farm payroll employment may not be as strong as it appears in the data.
From a structural perspective, layoffs in high-tech enterprises continue but at a lower level; while high-contact service sectors such as leisure hotels and education healthcare have seen significant job gains, data shows that there is still an existing labor shortage in the service sector.
With a rebound in services PMI, job openings and labor shortages data, and strong consumer credit data, we judge that US services have seen a rapid recovery after extreme weather and holidays.
We believe that the supply and demand tensions in the U.S. job market cannot be improved without a recession.
Currently, the US labor market has a shortfall of over 5 million in supply, and labor participation rates are rising slowly, with especially the labor participation rate for those aged 25-54 already approaching pre-pandemic highs.
Moreover, because of strong excess saving and consumption credit among US residents, the strength of consumer demand will further delay the impact on employment markets from tightening policies.
Persistent tightness in the US labor market supply and demand will be the backdrop to the US economy in 2023.
The overlap is now nearing the end of the rate hike, inflation expectations are repeating, and we are not optimistic about the continued decline in wage growth.
Before this data release, expectations for the Federal Reserve’s path of interest rate hikes in the US stock, bond, and currency markets were too optimistic.
As inflation data improve, the Fed has been tapering its tightening pace while maintaining the market’s fragile balance of expectations for an elevated rate of inflation; yet, in disregard of the Fed’s hawkish rhetoric, markets expect early stops to hiking and two further rate cuts at year-end.
With a tight labor market and uncertain impacts of contractionary policies, we consider the Federal Reserve’s guidance on interest rate hikes at year-end to be relatively reasonable. Adjustments in market extreme expectations and overcrowded trading may cause some harm to the market.
While this is not the case, stubborn wage growth has continued to impede the rapid decline in inflation that was observed recently. As M2 data turns negative and leading indicators of various CPI components gradually improve, US CPI data have turned towards a quick decline phase since October. Wage growth’s stubbornness will only provide support for future inflation data; current market expectations cannot be refuted. In the next round of inflation data releases, the market may enter another round of speculation.
A Quick Guide to Internet Finance
This book is quite superficial and could be considered an introductory course that helps people quickly get a grasp of the overall picture of internet finance, but for a deeper understanding, one still needs to follow the map and dig deeper on their own.
And given that the book was published earlier, the content about cultivating user habits through subsidies in later battles between Alipay and WeChat Pay has not been mentioned, it seems that the share of third-party payments today may not be as small as what was stated in the book.
Now, many people go out to eat with their phones, and merchants have also abandoned UnionPay due to high bank transaction volumes.
But still, I am very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book; it is evident that the author has had many years of financial observation experience and is familiar with both domestic and international financial development histories, which allows him/her to write in such an easy-to-understand manner.
Writing grandiloquently is easy, but writing in a way that is accessible and understandable is difficult.
